{"cuisine/andhra-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/andhra-cuisine","n":"Andhra cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Telugu cuisine","cuisine/telugu-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in India","intro":[["Andhra cuisine is the traditional culinary style of the Telugu people, who are native to the Indian state of Andhra Pradesh. It is generally known for its tangy, hot, and spicy taste."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"andhra-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andhra_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Mahanandi","ref":"site/nandyala-org","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"nandyala.org","note":"Indira's long-running blog of Andhra vegetarian home cooking.","url":"https://nandyala.org/mahanandi/archives/2006/12/12/cooking-at-home-with-pedatha-cookbook-review-interview-and-recipe/","suggested":true},{"name":"Spice Up The Curry (Andhra)","ref":"site/spiceupthecurry-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"spiceupthecurry.com","note":"Andhra section of Kanan Patel's Indian vegetarian recipe blog.","url":"https://www.spiceupthecurry.com/category/recipes-by-region/andhra-recipe/","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Five Morsels of Love","ref":"cookbook/five-morsels-of-love","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Archana Pidathala","note":"Telugu family recipes adapted from the author's grandmother's cookbook.","url":"","suggested":true},{"name":"Cooking at Home with Pedatha: Vegetarian Recipes from a Traditional Andhra Kitchen","ref":"cookbook/cooking-at-home-with-pedatha-vegetarian-recipes-from-a-traditional-andhra-kitchen","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Jigyasa Giri and Pratibha Jain","note":"Traditional Andhra vegetarian recipes recorded from an elder home cook.","url":"","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Telugu cuisine","cuisine/telugu-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/3/3e/Vegetarian_Andhra_Meal.jpg/500px-Vegetarian_Andhra_Meal.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"PriyaBooks","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vegetarian_Andhra_Meal.jpg"}},"cuisine/mon-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/mon-cuisine","n":"Mon cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Thailand","intro":[["The Mon are an ethnic group who inhabit Lower Myanmar's Mon State, Kayin State, Kayah State, Tanintharyi Region, Bago Region, the Irrawaddy Delta, and several areas in Thailand (mostly in Pathum Thani province, Phra Pradaeng and Nong Ya Plong). The native language is Mon, which belongs to the Monic branch of the Austroasiatic language family and shares a common origin with the Nyah Kur language, which is spoken by the people of the same name that live in Northeastern Thailand. A number of languages in Mainland Southeast Asia are influenced by the Mon language, which is also in turn influenced by those languages."],["The Mon were one of the earliest to reside in Southeast Asia, and were responsible for the spread of Theravada Buddhism in Mainland Southeast Asia. The civilizations founded by the Mon were some of the earliest in Thailand as well as Myanmar and Laos. The Mon are regarded as a large exporter of Southeast Asian culture. Historically, many cities in Myanmar, Thailand, and Laos today, including Yangon, Pathum Thani, Lamphun, Lampang, and Vientiane were founded either by the Mon people or Mon rulers."],["Nowadays, the Mon are a major ethnic group in Myanmar and a minor ethnic group in Thailand. The Mons from Myanmar are called Burmese Mon or Myanmar Mon. The Mons from Thailand are referred as Thai Raman or Thai Mon. The Mon dialects of Thailand and Myanmar are mutually intelligible."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"mon-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mon_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/3/32/Mon_people_girls_in_traditional_dress.jpg/500px-Mon_people_girls_in_traditional_dress.jpg","w":500,"h":760,"by":"Rockrangoon at English Wikipedia","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mon_people_girls_in_traditional_dress.jpg"}},"cuisine/crimean-tatar-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/crimean-tatar-cuisine","n":"Crimean Tatar cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Ukrainian cuisine","cuisine/ukrainian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Ukraine","intro":[["Crimean Tatar cuisine is primarily the cuisine of the Crimean Tatars, who live on the Crimean Peninsula. The traditional cuisine of the Crimean Tatars has similarities with that of Greeks, Italians, Balkan peoples, Nogais, North Caucasians, and Volga Tatars, although some national dishes and dietary habits vary between different Crimean Tatar regional subgroups; for example, fish and produce are more popular among Yaliboylu and Tat dishes while meat and dairy is more prevalent in Steppe Tatar cuisine. Many Uzbek dishes were incorporated into Crimean Tatar national cuisine during exile in Central Asia since 1944, and these dishes have become prevalent in Crimea since the return. Uzbek samsa, laghman, and plov (pilaf) are sold in most Tatar roadside cafes in Crimea as national dishes. In turn, some Crimean Tatar dishes, including Chiburekki, have been adopted by peoples outside Crimea, such as in Turkey and the North Caucasus."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"crimean-tatar-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crimean_Tatar_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Ukrainian cuisine","cuisine/ukrainian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Dishes","count":2,"links":[["Chebureki","dish/chebureki"],["Stuffed leaves","dish/stuffed-leaves"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Animal fats and oils","ingredient/animal-fats-and-oils"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Lamb","ingredient/lamb"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Cabbage","ingredient/cabbage"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Grape leaves","ingredient/grape-leaves"],["Ground meat","ingredient/ground-meat"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Ukrainian cuisine","cuisine/ukrainian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/e/ee/Burek.jpg/500px-Burek.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Loyna","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.5","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.5","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Burek.jpg"}},"dish/bacalhau-a-narcisa":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/bacalhau-a-narcisa","n":"Bacalhau à Narcisa","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Portuguese cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Bacalhau à Narcisa, also known as bacalhau à minhota or bacalhau à Braga, is a traditional Portuguese cod fish (bacalhau). It consists of fried cod loins, accompanied by thinly sliced potatoes and onions that are fried in the same olive oil as the cod, together with bay leaves and cloves."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-bacalhau-a-narcisa"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bacalhau_%C3%A0_Narcisa"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Portuguese cuisine","cuisine/portuguese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":6,"links":[["Bacalhau","ingredient/bacalhau"],["Fries","ingredient/fries"],["Olive","ingredient/olive"],["Olive oil","ingredient/olive-oil"],["Parsley","ingredient/parsley"],["Salt cod","ingredient/salt-cod"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Food From Portugal","ref":"site/foodfromportugal-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"foodfromportugal.com","note":"Portuguese-run re